WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.450
How to Analyze an Instructional Design Case.

2
00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:08.294
ID 553 - Module 2: ID Case Analysis I.

3
00:00:08.294 --> 00:00:14.362
Presented by Idowu David Awoyemi, The University of Alabama.

4
00:00:14.962 --> 00:00:18.485
Instructional design is a profession of judgment.

5
00:00:18.485 --> 00:00:21.002
Models tell you what steps to take.

6
00:00:21.002 --> 00:00:34.303
They don't tell you what to do when the client wants a
course but needs a job aid, when the subject-matter expert
won't return your calls, or when the budget disappears
halfway through.

7
00:00:34.303 --> 00:00:38.689
Case analysis develops the judgment that models can't
supply.

8
00:00:38.689 --> 00:00:48.108
A case is a realistic, incomplete, messy account of a design
situation, with more information than you need and less than
you want.

9
00:00:48.108 --> 00:00:53.429
Your task isn't to find the right answer, because there
usually isn't one.

10
00:00:53.429 --> 00:00:56.305
It's to construct a defensible analysis.

11
00:00:56.905 --> 00:01:00.964
Step one: summarize the situation in one paragraph.

12
00:01:00.964 --> 00:01:05.659
Who's involved, what's being asked, what's happened so far.

13
00:01:05.659 --> 00:01:07.569
Resist interpreting yet.

14
00:01:07.569 --> 00:01:12.185
Here's a short example we'll follow through all six steps.

15
00:01:12.185 --> 00:01:23.406
A school district asks a designer for an online course on
classroom management for first-year teachers, because new-
teacher turnover is high.

16
00:01:23.406 --> 00:01:32.559
Summary: district leadership requests a course; the stated
driver is turnover; the audience is first-year teachers.

17
00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:38.241
Step two: list every stakeholder, what they want, and what
they fear.

18
00:01:38.241 --> 00:02:04.167
In our example: district leaders want turnover down and want
something visible; principals want fewer discipline
referrals and don't want their teachers pulled out of class;
first-year teachers want to survive the year and fear being
judged; mentors have the tacit knowledge and no time;
students, who never appear in the request, want calm
classrooms.

19
00:02:04.167 --> 00:02:05.567
Note the conflicts.

20
00:02:05.567 --> 00:02:08.808
Leadership wants a course they can point to.

21
00:02:08.808 --> 00:02:13.227
Teachers need support in the moment, not a module in August.

22
00:02:13.827 --> 00:02:18.105
Step three is the heart of the analysis: frame the problem.

23
00:02:18.105 --> 00:02:22.963
The presenting request, 'build us a course', is rarely the
problem.

24
00:02:22.963 --> 00:02:29.416
Is there a performance gap, and is it caused by a lack of
knowledge or by something else?

25
00:02:29.416 --> 00:02:41.453
In our example: exit interviews might show new teachers
leave because of isolation and unmanageable class sizes, not
because they never learned management techniques.

26
00:02:41.453 --> 00:02:44.498
If so, a course addresses the wrong cause.

27
00:02:44.498 --> 00:02:52.474
State the problem in one or two sentences naming the gap,
the learners, and the constraint that makes it hard.

28
00:02:52.474 --> 00:02:56.535
And list what you don't know yet and how you'd find out.

29
00:02:57.135 --> 00:03:01.203
Step four: propose at least three courses of action.

30
00:03:01.203 --> 00:03:05.193
One should be the obvious one the client asked for.

31
00:03:05.193 --> 00:03:09.965
One should be non-instructional or only partly
instructional.

32
00:03:09.965 --> 00:03:15.442
One should be something a less experienced designer wouldn't
think of.

33
00:03:15.442 --> 00:03:29.680
In the example: the online course; a structured mentoring
program with release time; or a short just-in-time video
library paired with monthly problem-solving circles led by
mentors.

34
00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:36.487
For each, list benefits, costs, risks, and which
stakeholders would favor or resist it.

35
00:03:36.487 --> 00:03:38.677
Leadership likes the course.

36
00:03:38.677 --> 00:03:40.868
Principals like the circles.

37
00:03:40.868 --> 00:03:43.997
The budget office dislikes release time.

38
00:03:44.597 --> 00:03:47.118
Step five: recommend and justify.

39
00:03:47.118 --> 00:04:00.870
Choose one alternative, or a sequence, and justify it by
reference to the problem statement, the stakeholders, the
constraints, and at least one principle or model from the
course.

40
00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:04.002
Say what you'd do in the first two weeks.

41
00:04:04.002 --> 00:04:06.600
Step six: anticipate consequences.

42
00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:08.128
What could go wrong?

43
00:04:08.128 --> 00:04:11.947
What early indicators would tell you it's working?

44
00:04:11.947 --> 00:04:14.239
What would you do if it fails?

45
00:04:14.239 --> 00:04:30.130
In the example, a recommendation might be the circles plus
library first, with a needs assessment in the first two
weeks to test the isolation hypothesis, and referral data
and attendance as early indicators.

46
00:04:30.730 --> 00:04:33.759
Five mistakes sink most first analyses.

47
00:04:33.759 --> 00:04:40.437
Solving the presenting request: the client asked for a
course, so you design a course.

48
00:04:40.437 --> 00:04:49.990
Ignoring the learners: cases are told from the designer's or
client's side, so you have to ask what learners know and
need.

49
00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:57.367
Treating constraints as fixed when many are negotiable, as
long as you say how you'd negotiate.

50
00:04:57.367 --> 00:05:05.444
Recommending without trade-offs: if your recommendation has
no downside, you haven't looked hard enough.

51
00:05:05.444 --> 00:05:07.230
And retelling the case.

52
00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:10.958
Summarize briefly; spend your words on analysis.

53
00:05:11.558 --> 00:05:16.241
A strong analysis is specific to the case and cites evidence
from it.

54
00:05:16.241 --> 00:05:18.616
It applies course concepts by name.

55
00:05:18.616 --> 00:05:22.960
It reads like a memo a designer would actually hand to a
client.

56
00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:33.479
It runs about fifteen hundred to two thousand words, takes a
position, and defends it while acknowledging what a
reasonable colleague might do differently.

57
00:05:33.479 --> 00:05:44.269
Before you read the Crestview case, take a situation from
your own work where someone asked for training and run it
through steps two and three in ten minutes.

58
00:05:44.269 --> 00:05:46.102
Bring it to the discussion.
